Z||000006||rogergarrison||11-30-2001||06:36 AM||||the moldings take some extra care to prep. the texture is the prob. Id use paint designed for plastic to paint them. any anytime you paint something a diff color, if it does get chipped it does show up more. Also remember those strips are there to take the hits from other peoples car door, shopping carts, etc. something to think about.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||11-30-2001||08:56 AM||||I totally agree about the mouldings.  remember... its not my car and I didn't paint it.  I would have left the black from the start!  He did use flex agent in the paint on the whole car, so hopefully that keeps things flexible enough that it won't chip too bad.  Plus you can always touch up around the key whole.  Most cars today don't have the key hole in moulding at all, so the keys are scratching up the paint.  So its still ahead of the game!<P>I also love my red Fiero.  However, I do really like the yellow Formula's especially, and if the right car presented itself then I might consider selling my red one and getting a yellow Fiero.<P><P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/formulasig.JPG">           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">    <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||11-30-2001||11:41 AM||||Flex agent doesnt keep your paint flexible. It only keeps it flexible for a short period for reassembly. its gone in a couple weeks, evaporated.I never use it in my body shop and I do lots of high line cars, no complaints. sanding and prep is what makes it stay. If any paint store told you different, you been BS'd.<P>------------------<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/rogergarrison1.gif"> ||||reg||1
